    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Neonatal hyperbilirubinemia, caused by the accumulation of bilirubin in the circulation, occurs in roughly 60% of all newborn infants during the first week of life. Phototherapy using blue light with a bandwidth of 430–490 nm (peak of 450 nm) is currently the most common treatment, and photoalters bilirubin into compounds that can be excreted in the bile and urine. However, recent studies suggest that 500-nm light may be more efficacious at photoaltering bilirubin. Hemoglobin (Hb) has significant light absorption at 450 nm, nearly identical to that of bilirubin, and thus may act as a major competitor of bilirubin for absorbing light. To this end, I tested the effect of Hb [by varying hematocrit (Hct) levels] on in vitro bilirubin photoalteration under lights at 450 and 500 nm. I found that the presence of red blood cells (RBCs) significantly reduced the degree of bilirubin photoalteration equally for 450- and 500-nm light. In addition, I observed that 500-nm light was more effective at photoaltering bilirubin when no Hb was present, and this trend continued as the Hct increased. Based on these in vitro findings, I then performed studies to evaluate the in vivo efficacy and safety of 500-nm light using Gunn rat pups, which are genetically jaundiced. Efficacy was assessed through calculations of the rate of bilirubin photoalteration before and after light exposures. Side effects were assessed by measurements of total body excretion rates of carbon monoxide (CO) and carbon dioxide (CO2) and weight loss during exposures. I found that exposure to 500-nm light was safe and effective in altering bilirubin. Results of these in vitro and in vivo studies may not only change current phototherapy treatment guidelines, but may also provide insight into the design of safer and more efficacious phototherapy light sources for use in the treatment of neonatal hyperbilirubinemia.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
